…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ}And after three months we departed in a ship of Alexandria, which had wintered in the isle, whose sign was Castor and Pollux.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ5And landing at Syracuse, we tarried there three days.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ†And from thence we fetched a compass, and came to Rhegium: and after one day the south wind blew, and we came the next day to Puteoli:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙdwhere we found brethren, and were desired to tarry with them seven days: and so we went toward Rome.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¨And from thence, when the brethren heard of us, they came to meet us as far as Appii forum, and The three taverns: whom when Paul saw, he thanked God, and took courage.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses‘„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¤And when we came to Rome, the centurion delivered the prisoners to the captain of the guard: but Paul was suffered to dwell by himself with a soldier that kept him.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÚ6And it came to pass, that after three days Paul called the chief of the Jews together: and when they were come together, he said unto them, Men and brethren, though I have committed nothing against the people, or customs of our fathers, yet was I delivered prisoner from Jerusalem into the hands of the Romans.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ`Who, when they had examined me, would have let me go, because there was no cause of death in me.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙyBut when the Jews spake against it, I was constrained to appeal unto CÃ¦sar; not that I had ought to accuse my nation of.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ’For this cause therefore have I called for you, to see you, and to speak with you: because that for the hope of Israel I am bound with this chain.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙšAnd they said unto him, We neither received letters out of JudÃ¦a concerning thee, neither any of the brethren that came shewed or spake any harm of thee.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ}But we desire to hear of thee what thou thinkest: for as concerning this sect, we know that every where it is spoken against.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses—„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙúAnd when they had appointed him a day, there came many to him into his lodging; to whom he expounded and testified the kingdom of God, persuading them concerning Jesus, both out of the law of Moses, and out of the prophets, from morning till evening.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFAnd some believed the things which were spoken, and some believed not.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ And when they agreed not among themselves, they departed, after that Paul had spoken one word, Well spake the Holy Ghost by Esaias the prophet unto our fathers,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines”‚¤text§saying,¦layout ‚¤text½Go unto this people, and say,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ0Hearing ye shall hear, and shall not understand;¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ*And seeing ye shall see, and not perceive:¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines˜‚¤textÙ,For the heart of this people is waxed gross,¦layout ‚¤textÙ#And their ears are dull of hearing,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ And their eyes have they closed;¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ%Lest they should see with their eyes,¦layout§quote_1‚¤text¹And hear with their ears,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ And understand with their heart,¦layout§quote_1‚¤text¸And should be converted,¦layout§quote_1‚¤text·And I should heal them.¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙpBe it known therefore unto you, that the salvation of God is sent unto the Gentiles, and that they will hear it.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ^And when he had said these words, the Jews departed, and had great reasoning among themselves.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ^And Paul dwelt two whole years in his own hired house, and received all that came in unto him,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙˆpreaching the kingdom of God, and teaching those things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ, with all confidence, no man forbidding him.¦layout ªlast_verse